\begin{invariant}[Block persistence]\label{inv:blocks}
After every batch, with $\nb$ the current block count: \textup{(i)} for
every $x<\nb$, $M^{-1}(x)$ is the vertex set of a biconnected component of
the prefix seen so far and $x$ is a \emph{single} node of $H$;
\textup{(ii)} for every $x \ge \nb$, $|M^{-1}(x)| = 1$.
\end{invariant}

\noindent
Part~(ii) is what makes the last line of Alg.~\ref{alg:sp} well defined;
part~(i) is what lets the one-word test $x<\nb$ stand in for ``$x$ is a
finished component''. A block, once formed, is never split:
\textsc{De-Merge} removes a \emph{member} and gives it a fresh id above the
watermark, leaving the block node intact.

\begin{lemma}[Un-fencing merges at the parent]\label{lem:parent}
Let $(c,p) \in T$ with $p=\pare(c)$, and let $l[x]$ be the class of $x$'s
parent edge. Including $(c,p)$ in $G'$ forces $l[c]=l[p]$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Classes are read off the components of $G'$ at the deeper endpoint, so
$l[x]=\mathit{rep}[x]$. Adding $(c,p)$ to $G'$ puts $c$ and $p$ in one
component, hence $\mathit{rep}[c]=\mathit{rep}[p]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The predicate is forced]\label{cor:forced}
Every edge at a block $B$ must take one class. By Lemma~\ref{lem:parent}
the merge lands at the \emph{parent}, so $B$ must be the parent: the test is
$\pare(e)<\nb$. $B$'s own up-edge needs no union: $B$ is the child there, so
that edge already carries class $l[B]$ by definition of $l$.
\end{corollary}

\noindent
This rules out the neighbouring predicates. Un-fencing \emph{the de-merge
links} lets the arbitrary rooting of $T$ decide which endpoint is the
parent, so the merge lands on the leaver whenever the leaver is rooted above
the block; un-fencing \emph{every} tree edge at a block also un-fences $B$'s
up-edge, whose merge lands at $\pare(B)$, typically a genuine cut vertex;
and restricting to \emph{freshly} de-merged links is round-local while the
link persists for the rest of the stream.

\paragraph{Where $\nb$ is consulted.}
Invariant~\ref{inv:blocks} is discharged at four comparisons against the
watermark: in \textsc{Compress-Graph}, (1)~a class meeting a carried block
is structural, so a block with one attachment is not demoted to an
own-node, (2)~a carried block with no class of its own is still a block,
(3)~an unclaimed id is an own-node iff it is not a carried block; and
(4)~in \textsc{PlainEdges}, a tree edge whose parent is a carried block is
never fenced.
